#{[#edb/ref 17592186050136 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060584} :activity/id #uuid "b1ce0b0f-b6c2-5cc2-9063-1be68a8f54d5" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060907 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given two vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -4,4 \\rangle\n$$\n\nshown below. How do we compute the vectors $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$ and $-5\\mathbf{a}$ in terms of the standard basis $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$?\n\n![](images/25/254809cbc834b493759a6fa09222a2c90c5fbfc3280d22e82470e760a7a4c508.png)\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$, we collect all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s. We know that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = {\\color{red}2}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}-4}\\mathbf{i} + {\\color{blue}4}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= (2i + 3j) + (-4i + 4j) \\\\\n&= (2 + (-4))i + (3 + 4)j \\\\\n&=-2i + 7j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo compute $-5\\mathbf{a}$, we multiply each of the components by $-5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot a &= -5(2i + 3j) \\\\\n&= (-5 \\cdot 2)i + (-5 \\cdot 3)j \\\\\n&=-10i + - 15j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our answer in angle bracket and column vector notations, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059523 :question/id #uuid "1d2717f9-b905-59eb-85d7-6db4f9c9dc7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4292" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$ if $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=12\\mathbf{i}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 2b &= (-4i + 6j) - 2(12i) \\\\\n&=-4i + 6j - 24i \\\\\n&= (-4i - 24i) + 6j \\\\\n&=-28i + 6j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Component Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060908 :tutorial/content "To add or subtract vectors given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to add or subtract each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨2, 5⟩ + ⟨- 3, 7⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨2 + (-3), 5 + 7⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨- 1, 12⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 + (-3) \\\\ 5 + 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, to multiply a vector given in angle bracket or column vector form, we just need to multiply each component separately.\n\nFor example, in angle bracket notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot ⟨2, 5⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨(-5) \\cdot 2, (-5) \\cdot 5⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨- 10,-25⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand in column vector notation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}(-5) \\cdot 2 \\\\ (-5) \\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Angle Bracket or Column Vector Form"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059524 :question/id #uuid "2cb63c87-320a-5cb4-b413-8c6bf9723641" :question/math-academy-id "e-4293" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -7, 15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 14, -11 \\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 3b &= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - 3 \\cdot ⟨14,-11⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - ⟨42,-33⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7 - 42, 15 - (-33)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 49, 48⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors Using Angle Bracket Notation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059525 :question/id #uuid "43cfef07-255d-5167-b815-267883918993" :question/math-academy-id "e-4294" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $2\\mathbf{a}-(\\mathbf{b}-5\\mathbf{c})$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - (b - 5c) &= 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} - (\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - 5 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} - (\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -31\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Column Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060909 :tutorial/content "To denote a vector, we have used two notations:\n\n- a single bold lowercase letter, such as $\\mathbf{v}$, or\n- two uppercase letters representing the points of the ends of the vector, with an arrow over them, such as $\\overrightarrow{AB}$.\n\nWe will mostly stick to these notations. However, there are many possible notations for vectors. For example, sometimes a vector can be denoted as\n$\\vec{v}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{V}, \\quad v, \\quad \\hat{v}$.\nThe rightmost notation, $\\hat{v}$, is usually used to represent unit vectors in particular." :tutorial/title "Common Notations Used When Describing Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059526 :question/id #uuid "0adf8fad-9a6f-5eb1-970f-11633ba98ca1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4295" :question/problem "The vectors $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are given in the picture below. Find the coordinates of $\\vec{a}+6\\vec{b}$.\n\n![](images/56/56b10e759084da6fb21c8421bc9ff6d74aac2602df28f6c8e478bd874d7d6e9f.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}=\\langle -3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\vec{b}=\\langle 4,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, to compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^(→) + 6b^(→) &= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + 6 \\cdot ⟨4, 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨6 \\cdot 4, 6 \\cdot 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨24, 30⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨21, 32⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors Given in Alternative Notation"}}] :activity/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Cartesian Vectors in 2D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059922}] [#edb/ref 17592186050027 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125298} :activity/id #uuid "00d7af21-acdd-52ae-8ffc-37290b0f0c74" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125735 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$*\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental link between calculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)= x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$ It is easy to see that $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$*\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, $$\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.$$\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$ is parallel to the level curve.\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain $$\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if the gradient vector is nonzero, i.e.,\n\n$$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function\n\n$$w = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).$$\n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$\n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\\eqalign{ \\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt] &= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt] &= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt] \\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle. }$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" :tutorial/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector For Functions of More than Two Variables"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124731}] [#edb/ref 17592186050031 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167577} :activity/id #uuid "37fac92b-9502-52a2-8069-399fccbd8a62" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166586 :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification."} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166588 :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z},$ where $x = s + t,$ $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{xy+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(s-t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(s+t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}={e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 1+(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-1)+{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =(s-t-s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168061 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for single variable functions $f=f(x)$ and $x=x(t)$ states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nLet's now suppose that $f = f(x,y),$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$ are single-variable functions. How do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d t}$ in this case?\n\nThe functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are known as **intermediate variables.** They are independent variables for the function $f(x,y),$ but are themselves dependent on the variable $t.$\n\nOne way to consider how $f(x,y)$ depends on $t$ is to draw a tree diagram.\n\nStarting with $f(x,y),$ we draw a branch for each intermediate variable. Then, we draw a branch for both $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ which are both dependent on the variable $t.$\n\n![](images/cd/cdc47e038ecaba62ae612a0ac0bc07067af232353fc4fc169bf4be57b3abbd1f.png)\n\nWe then label each branch with the derivative describing how the function changes due to a change in the variable. \n\n![](images/ec/ece6012e358b5b6920395df88acd8d7037a7b15bed1add1220365ba72bd24cbd.png)\n\nThen, traversing each branch, we compute the product of the derivatives along each branch and place the result at the end of the branch.\n\n![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nFinally, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we sum the results for each branch:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}\n = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThis result is called the **multivariable chain rule**." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168062 :tutorial/content "How do we apply the multivariable chain rule if the intermediate variables are also dependent on two variables? \n\nFor example, suppose we have\n\n$$\nf = f(x,y), \\qquad x=g(s,t), \\qquad y=h(s,t).\n$$\n\nHow do we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}?$ \n\nFirst, let's construct a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/ca/ca524eda2e6be3354fcb72bed52fcaf22257073c3228d74850f8226f9ef0af6f.png)\n\nNow, since we're interested in $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s},$ we highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $s:$\n\n![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nIf we keep $t$ fixed and differentiate $f$ with respect to $s,$ then according to the chain rule for two intermediate variables and one independent variable, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial s} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we wanted to compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t},$ we would highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nKeeping $s$ fixed and differentiating $f$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Two Independent Variables"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168063 :tutorial/content "We can generalize the chain rule to a multivariable function containing an arbitrary number of intermediate and independent variables.\n\nConsider the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf = f(x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_m)\n$$\n\nwith $m$ independent variables and, for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots, n\\},$ the variable \n\n$$\nx_i = g_i(t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_n)\n$$\n\nis a differentiable function of $n$ independent variables. Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t_j} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_1}{\\partial t_j} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_2}{\\partial t_j} + \\dots + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_m}{\\partial t_j}\n$$\n\nfor $j \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots, n\\}.$" :tutorial/title "The Generalized Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168088 :tutorial/content "There is a special case of the multivariable chain rule when $f=f(x,y)$ and $y=g(x).$ \n\nNotice that $x$ is an independent variable here. Therefore, the chain rule becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}}_{⏟}}_{1}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "A Special Case of the Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167574}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166978}] [#edb/ref 17592186049991 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176867} :activity/id #uuid "b5e5427c-d46e-5198-b0d9-c69a155fe586" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177070 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of the cosecant function again.\n\n![](images/67/67463d29b77a6db10538bcf5956f9bb8a40a5d41a2b7741c6de52bd336bcc550.png)\n\nThe graph of the cosecant function has infinitely many asymptotes, and there is an infinite number of points where the function takes values of $-1$ and $1.$\n\nLet's write general expressions for these properties, by assuming $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nThe function has asymptotes when $x=0,\\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots,$ etc. Therefore, a general equation for the asymptotes of the graph is\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $1$ when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Since the function has a period of $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\csc x = 1$ is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{-1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $-1$ when $x=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Since the function has a period $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\csc x = -1$ is\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +2n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175699 :question/id #uuid "12dc1c50-69bc-54d8-8d60-a7fc144c9644" :question/math-academy-id "e-11008" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the graph of $y=\\csc x?$\n\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x = -\\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n\n- The function has zeroes at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\csc x.$\n\n![](images/a9/a92d46319d381f0a8cab7823021b3b7e9b5c030ad964586b8e72129a2cd5abe7.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x = \\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n.$ Therefore, statement I is false.\n\n- The range of $y = \\csc x$ is $(-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty).$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The cosecant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."} :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177071 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of the function $y=\\csc x$ once more.\n\n![](images/67/67463d29b77a6db10538bcf5956f9bb8a40a5d41a2b7741c6de52bd336bcc550.png)\n\nAs previously mentioned, the cosecant function inherits its periodicity from the sine function, so the period is $2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce59afb77eb33880d7e92d1c294b872ec3af898c43a2af6816ef63fee64abd3b.png)\n\nThe periodicity of $y=\\csc x$ means that $\\csc (x+2n\\pi ) = \\csc x$ for any number $x$ in the domain of $\\csc x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176054 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175700 :question/id #uuid "a1713375-241f-5a53-a109-3c3d0f917411" :question/math-academy-id "e-11009" :question/problem "If $\\csc x = 8,$ then what is $\\csc(x-8\\pi)?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\csc x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\csc{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-4)\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\csc{x} = 8,$ then $\\csc{(x-8\\pi)} = 8.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176060 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175706 :question/id #uuid "12eb5678-b62b-50ad-97bf-88768c4bf365" :question/math-academy-id "e-11015" :question/problem "If $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{7}{2},$ then find the value of $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y = \\csc{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\csc{x}$ is $2\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\csc{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\csc{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-20\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\csc{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\csc(-x - 20\\pi) = -\\dfrac{7}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177086 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosecant is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the cosecant function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/cb/cb63f7c0930524e9f54a65aad6d74784c403a6255f723b879d76586ad8ea598b.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of cosecant in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/title "The Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176878}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Cosecant Function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176117}]}
